Location: app/assets/javascripts/discourse/controllers/create_account_controller.js:69-74 (RIGHT)

Rejected-email reason always shown as generic 'invalid' regardless of actual server-side failure reason

Trigger: Server rejects an email for any reason (blocked, blacklisted domain, or already taken) and the client re-renders emailValidation for that same value.

Impact: Users see a generic 'invalid' message for emails that were actually rejected because they are already registered, blacklisted, or blocked, obscuring the real reason and creating confusing UX (e.g., an already-registered user cannot tell they should log in instead).

Evidence: if (this.get('rejectedEmails').contains(email)) {
      return Discourse.InputValidation.create({
        failed: true,
        reason: I18n.t('user.email.invalid')
      });
    }

Suggested fix: Preserve and surface the specific server-provided error message/reason instead of hardcoding I18n.t('user.email.invalid').

Location: app/assets/javascripts/discourse/controllers/create_account_controller.js:274-276 (RIGHT)

No test coverage for new rejectedEmails client-side tracking behavior

Trigger: A signup request fails with an email-related server error.

Impact: The new client-side logic that records rejected emails and re-validates emailValidation against them has no automated test, so regressions (e.g., the shared-array bug) would not be caught by CI.

Evidence: if (result.errors && result.errors.email && result.values) {
          createAccountController.get('rejectedEmails').pushObject(result.values.email);
        }

Suggested fix: Add a QUnit/Ember test that simulates a failed createAccount response with result.errors.email and result.values.email, and asserts rejectedEmails/emailValidation update correctly and are scoped per controller instance.

