            const fetchTokens = await myGoogleAuth.refreshToken(googleCredentials.refresh_token);
            return fetchTokens.res;
          },
          "google-calendar",
          credential.userId
        );

Suggested fix: Normalize the sync response (e.g. `await response.json()`) inside refreshOAuthTokens or in this caller before reading token fields.

Location: packages/app-store/salesforce/lib/CalendarService.ts:75-84 (RIGHT)

Salesforce token refresh runs unconditionally on every client construction and the refreshed token is never used by the returned connection

Trigger: Every instantiation of SalesforceCalendarService (constructor calls getClient), regardless of whether the stored access token is still valid.

Impact: Each calendar operation issues a refresh-token grant to Salesforce (extra latency, API/rate-limit consumption), yet the jsforce Connection below is still constructed with the stale `credentialKey.access_token` rather than `accessTokenParsed.data.access_token`, so the newly fetched token is only written to the DB and never used in-session.

Evidence: const response = await fetch("https://login.salesforce.com/services/oauth2/token", {
      method: "POST",
      body: new URLSearchParams({
        grant_type: "refresh_token",
        client_id: consumer_key,
        client_secret: consumer_secret,
        refresh_token: credentialKey.refresh_token,
        format: "json",
      }),
    });

Suggested fix: Only refresh when the stored token is expired, and pass the refreshed access_token (and instance_url) into the jsforce Connection.

