Location: packages/features/ee/workflows/api/scheduleSMSReminders.ts:178-186 (RIGHT)

SMS-lock state increments retryCount and leads to permanent deletion of a locked account's reminders

Trigger: A user or team is temporarily in SMSLockState.LOCKED: twilio.scheduleSMS returns undefined (its early return on isLockedForSMSSending), so the else branch runs on two consecutive cron executions.

Impact: Every pending SMS reminder for the locked account reaches retryCount 2 within two cron runs and is deleted by the next run's deleteMany; if the lock is later lifted (e.g. a false-positive spam lock is reviewed), the reminders are already gone and cannot be sent.

Evidence: } else {
          await prisma.workflowReminder.update({
            where: {
              id: reminder.id,
            },
            data: {
              retryCount: reminder.retryCount + 1,
            },
          });

Suggested fix: Distinguish the lock case from a genuine scheduling failure — e.g. have scheduleSMS signal 'locked' distinctly and skip the retryCount increment (or skip locked reminders in the query) so reminders survive a temporary lock.

Location: packages/features/ee/workflows/api/scheduleSMSReminders.ts:190-197 (RIGHT)

Transient failures (rate limit, Twilio outage) count toward retryCount and permanently delete valid reminders

Trigger: twilio.scheduleSMS throws for a transient reason — checkSMSRateLimit throwing on a monthly rate limit, a temporary Twilio/network outage, or missing env credentials — on two consecutive cron executions for the same reminder.

Impact: The catch block increments retryCount for every error indiscriminately; after two transient failures the deleteMany at the top of the next run permanently deletes the reminder, so a valid SMS with a correct phone number is never sent even after the outage or rate limit clears.

Evidence: await prisma.workflowReminder.update({
        where: {
          id: reminder.id,
        },
        data: {
          retryCount: reminder.retryCount + 1,
        },
      });

Suggested fix: Only count permanent, non-retryable failures (e.g. Twilio invalid-number error codes) toward retryCount, or raise the deletion threshold and skip incrementing on known-transient errors such as rate-limit and network exceptions.

