Location: services/src/main/java/org/keycloak/services/resources/admin/permissions/GroupPermissionsV2.java:121-124 (RIGHT)

getGroupIdsWithViewPermission() uses Resource id instead of group id

Trigger: A caller without global user-view permission but with a group-scoped view-members/manage-members permission triggers a user search (e.g. via UsersResource/BruteForceUsersResource) that calls auth.groups().getGroupIdsWithViewPermission().

Impact: hasPermission() is called with groupResource.getId() (the internal Resource UUID) instead of the resource name (which stores the actual GroupModel id, as created via resolveGroup()/group.getId()); the findByName lookup inside hasPermission therefore never matches the specific group resource and evaluation falls back to the 'all-groups' policy. Additionally the granted set is populated with resource ids rather than GroupModel ids, so it never matches the actual group ids used later (session.setAttribute(UserModel.GROUPS, ...)) to filter users by group, silently breaking group-scoped user visibility.

Evidence: resourceStore.findByType(server, AdminPermissionsSchema.GROUPS_RESOURCE_TYPE, groupResource -> {
            if (hasPermission(groupResource.getId(), AdminPermissionsSchema.VIEW_MEMBERS, AdminPermissionsSchema.MANAGE_MEMBERS)) {
                granted.add(groupResource.getId());
            }
        });

Suggested fix: Use groupResource.getName() (which stores the GroupModel id) instead of groupResource.getId() both when calling hasPermission() and when adding to the granted set.

